Port WinUAE 5.3.1's Picasso96 module onto this tree

libretro-uae ships sources/src/picasso96.c, but it is the UAE 0.8.x / P-UAE one, while every
other file around it is WinUAE 5.3.1 (README.md says so, and gfxboard.c, expansion.c, drawing.c
and custom.c all use the 5.3.1 shape). The two do not meet: the old module declares its own
gfxmemory/gfxmem_mask bank and a one-argument gfx_set_picasso_state(), and drawing.c calls
gfx_set_picasso_state(monid, on) and picasso_enablescreen(monid, on) against a
picasso96_state[] array the old header does not have. Defining PICASSO96 over it does not
compile, which is very likely why it was never defined.

So the module is replaced by the one the rest of the tree was written against: WinUAE's
od-win32/picasso96_win.cpp and od-win32/picasso96_win.h, at the same commit the README pins,
plus p96_blit.cpp which picasso96_win.cpp #includes 61 times to generate its blitters. The
@vendor-picasso96 phase fetches those three; this patch is everything that has to change in
them to build here, and it is deliberately small - the RTG logic itself is untouched.

What changes, and why:

* od-win32 headers. registry.h, win32gui.h, resource.h, win32gfx.h and direct3d.h do not exist
  outside WinUAE's Windows front end. Nothing in this module reads the registry, so that include
  simply goes; the rest is replaced by retrodep/picasso_host.h, which declares the same surface
  (WIN32GFX_GetWidth/Height, DX_Invalidate, gfx_lock/unlock, default_freq, CURSORMAXWIDTH, the
  three D3D_ function pointers) for the libretro front end to implement.

* render.h -> misc.h. picasso96_win.h pulls struct PicassoResolution, struct MultiDisplay and
  struct AmigaMonitor out of od-win32/render.h, which starts by including d3d9.h. This tree
  already has portable versions of all three in sources/src/include/misc.h - see patch 1320.

* ../p96_blit.cpp -> p96_blit.inc. WinUAE's copy of this module lives in od-win32/, so the
  blitter generator is one directory up. Here it is beside it, and .inc is this tree's suffix
  for a source that is #included rather than compiled (adm_palette_vis.inc and the rest).

* The Windows mouse pointer. putwinmousepixel(), createwindowscursor() and
  picasso_setwincursor() pushed the Amiga sprite into the host window as an HCURSOR through GDI
  - HBITMAP, HDC, ICONINFO, SetCursor. A libretro core has no window of its own, so all three
  become stubs. Nothing is lost on screen: D3D_setcursor is NULL here, so hwsprite stays 0,
  uaegfx never claims BIF_HARDWARESPRITE, and Picasso96 draws the pointer into the frame buffer
  itself.

* Windows types. ULONG_PTR, PVOID, SIZE_T, CRITICAL_SECTION and Sleep() come from windows.h.
  This core builds on macOS and Linux too, so the write-watch API moves to plain types (see
  patch 1340), the render lock becomes the tree's own uae_sem_t, and Sleep() becomes
  uae_msleep(). _cdecl on a qsort comparator is dropped.

* natmem_offset. WinUAE reserves one window for the whole Amiga address space, so a board's host
  pointer is natmem_offset + its Amiga address - which is what this file wrote in six places.
  NATMEM_OFFSET is only defined under JIT (sysdeps.h) and this core has no JIT, so
  mapped_malloc() hands each bank an unrelated xcalloc block and natmem_offset stays NULL. The
  same expression would then produce the Amiga address cast to a pointer and the first flush
  would read from $200000. rtg_vram() returns the bank's own baseaddr, which is right either
  way. The max_physmem guard around it goes with it: it exists to refuse a board mapped outside
  that window, and there is no window here.

* GetSystemInfo()/GetWriteWatch(). Replaced by mman_GetWriteWatch() and a page size both ends
  agree on - see patch 1340.

* frame_drawn() is guarded with #ifdef AVIOUTPUT, matching the identical call in drawing.c.
  AVIOUTPUT is not defined in this build (the host records its own video).

* Two forward declarations become static, matching the definitions below them. MSVC accepts the
  mismatch; gcc -std=gnu99 does not.

Applies to the files installed by @vendor-picasso96, which fetches them from WinUAE
51b7be351f359dc97f967f051da390283bc4b409.

One thing here is not a port detail but a real defect, found by the first machine that tried to
start: gfxmem_banks[] is now initialised statically rather than in InitPicasso96(). WinUAE fills
it there, and gets away with it because reset_drawing() calls InitPicasso96() early enough. In
this port nothing calls it before memory_init(), and expansion_init() walks all four slots
calling mapped_free(gfxmem_banks[i]) on the way - four null dereferences, and a segfault before
any machine ran at all. The four banks are file-scope objects a few lines above, so pointing at
them at their definition costs nothing and cannot be too late.

